                      %a{:href => "#empty_type_2"} Empty type
                    %li
                      %a{:href => "#unit_type_2"} Unit type
                    %li
                      %a{:href => "#booleans_type_2"} Booleans type
                    %li
                      %a{:href => "#natural_numbers_type_2"} Natural numbers type
              %li
                %a{:href => "#categorical_semantics"}  Categorical semantics
              %li
                %a{:href => "#open_problems"} Open problems
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"}  References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            where the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computation+rules"} computation rules
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/uniqueness+rules"} uniqueness rules
            for types use
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+types"} identity types
            instead of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgmental+equality"} judgmental equality
          %p The dependent type theory has many different names in the existing literature
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %em axiomatic type theory
                (e.g.
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "#Otten24"} Otten 2024
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#Spadetto25"} Spadetto 2025
            %li
              %p
                %em propositional type theory
                (e.g.
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#Spadetto23"} Spadetto 2023
            %li
              %p
                %em objective type theory
                (e.g.
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#BB21"} van den Berg &amp; den Besten 2021
            %li
              %p
                %em weak type theory
                (e.g.
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#Winterhalter20"} Winterhalter 2020
          %p
            Propositional type theory has
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/decidable"} decidable
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+checking"} type checking
          %h2#definitions_in_propositional_type_theory Definitions in propositional type theory
          %p
            Propositional type theories come into two general versions, depending on how they treat
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/definitions"} definitions
            in dependent type theory:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Dependent type theories which have a judgmental
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/definitional+equality"} definitional equality
                (e.g.
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "#Spadetto23"} Spadetto 2023
            %li
              %p
                Dependent type theories which do not have judgmental
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/definitional+equality"} definitional equality
                (e.g.
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "#BB21"} van den Berg &amp; den Besten 2021
          %p These two approaches differ in whether aliases of terms and types reduce to the original term or type, or are identified or equivalent to the original term or type. They each have their own advantages:
          %p Advantages of not having judgmental definitional equality include
          %ul
            %li
              %p a simpler set of inference rules
            %li
              %p more models
            %li
              %p type theories without any definitional equalities are cofibrant in categories of theories.
          %p Advantages of having judgmental definitional equality include
          %ul
            %li
              %p shorter internal proofs and constructions
            %li
              %p avoids “higher transport hell” for aliases of terms and types
          %h3#with_judgmental_definitional_equality With judgmental definitional equality
          %p
            For propositional type theory with judgmental definitional equality, definitions of aliases of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/types"} types
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terms"} terms
            are still made using
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgmental+equality"} judgmental equality
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+rules"} structural rules
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/congruence+rules"} congruence rules
            have to be postulated explicitly in the theory like in the usual dependent type theories. In addition to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/congruence+rules"} congruence rules
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgmental+equality"} judgmental equality
            for the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formation+rules"} formation rules
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/introduction+rules"} introduction rules
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elimination+rules"} elimination rules
            of each type former, there are additional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/congruence+rules"} congruence rules
            for the computation and uniqueness rules, since the conversion rules are represented by the structure of an identification rather than judgmental equality, and thus this structure has to be preserved across judgmental equality.
          %h3#without_judgmental_definitional_equality Without judgmental definitional equality
          %p
            For propositional type theory without judgmental definitional equality,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/definitions"} definitions
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/types"} types
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terms"} terms
            are made using
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identifications"} identifications
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalences+of+types"} equivalences of types
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≡
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \equiv
            in the theory represents
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/alpha-equivalence"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
              \-equivalence
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntactic+equality"} syntactic equality
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+rules"} structural rules
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/congruence+rules"} congruence rules
            are
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/admissible+rules"} admissible rules
          %p
            Furthermore, in the context of propositional type theory where there is no
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/definitional+equality"} definitional equality
            in the type theory in the traditional sense, there is the question of how to define aliases of terms and types in the type theory.
          %p
            For the case of terms, that is easily resolved by using identity types. For example, to define
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            as the successor of the succcessor of zero in the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+type"} natural numbers type
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identification"} identification
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} def
                    %mn 2
                    %mo :
                    %mn 2
                    %msub
                      %mo =
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} N
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{def}2:2 =_{\mathrm{N}} s(s(0))
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isProp"} isProp
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modality"} modality
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} isProp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{isProp}(A)
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            indicating whether the type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mere+proposition"} mere proposition
            is usually defined to be
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo :
                        %mi A
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi y
                        %mo :
                        %mi A
                    %msub
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Id
                      %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prod_{x:A} \prod_{y:A} \mathrm{Id}_A(x, y)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgmental+equality"} judgmental equality
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} isProp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≡
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Id
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{isProp}(A) \equiv \prod_{x:A} \prod_{y:A} \mathrm{Id}_A(x, y)
          %p
            When presenting
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            using
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Russell+universes"} Russell universes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/typal+equality"} typal equality
            instead, because every type is an element of a Russell universe, and so one could write
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} defisProp
                    %mi A
                  %mo :
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} isProp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mo =
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Type
                        %mi i
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Id
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{defisProp}_A:\mathrm{isProp}(A) =_{\mathrm{Type}_i} \prod_{x:A} \prod_{y:A} \mathrm{Id}_A(x, y)
          %p
            for types
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} isProp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Type
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{isProp}(A):\mathrm{Type}_i
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo :
                        %mi A
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi y
                        %mo :
                        %mi A
                    %msub
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Id
                      %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Type
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prod_{x:A} \prod_{y:A} \mathrm{Id}_A(x, y):\mathrm{Type}_i
          %p On the other hand, when using a separate type judgment, types are not elements of other types, and thus one cannot compare them for typal equality. Instead, one has two alternatives
          %ul
            %li
              One can use
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polymorphic+dependent+type+theory"} polymorphic dependent type theory
              with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+variables"} type variables
              and postulate an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+type#IdentityTypeBetweenTypes"} identity type between types
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo =
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A = B
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} defisProp
                        %mi A
                      %mo :
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} isProp
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mo :
                          %mi A
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                        %mrow
                          %mi y
                          %mo :
                          %mi A
                      %msub
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Id
                        %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{defisProp}_A:\mathrm{isProp}(A) = \prod_{x:A} \prod_{y:A} \mathrm{Id}_A(x, y)
            %li
              One can postulate a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+of+equivalences"} type of equivalences
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \simeq B
              as a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/record+type"} record type
              with propositional computation rules, where we then have
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} defisProp
                        %mi A
                      %mo :
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} isProp
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≃
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mo :
                          %mi A
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                        %mrow
                          %mi y
                          %mo :
                          %mi A
                      %msub
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Id
                        %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{defisProp}_A:\mathrm{isProp}(A) \simeq \prod_{x:A} \prod_{y:A} \mathrm{Id}_A(x, y)
          %h2#formalizations_and_syntax Formalizations and syntax
          %p
            In section 9 of
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Winterhalter20"} Winterhalter 2020
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Theo+Winterhalter"} Theo Winterhalter
            indicates that there are many ways to formalize
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Russell+universes"} Russell universes
            or a separate
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgment"} judgment
            to denote types.
            %a{:href => "#BB21"} van den Berg &amp; den Besten 2021
            and
            %a{:href => "#Spadetto23"} Spadetto 2023
            for example both use
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Russell+universes"} Russell universes
            in their formal presentation of dependent type theory.
          %p
            In this section, we present two separate formalizations, the first using a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universe+hierarchy"} universe hierarchy
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Russell+universes"} Russell universes
            in the style of
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#UFP13"} UFP 13
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Rijke22"} Rijke 2022
          %h3#without_a_separate_type_judgment Without a separate type judgment
          %p
            In this section, we describe a formalization of propositional type theory using an infinite hierarchy of Russell universes with cumulativity, in the style of
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#UPF13"} UPF 2013
          %h4#judgments_contexts_and_universes Judgments, contexts, and universes
          %p This presentation of propositional type theory consists of the following judgments:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Element judgments, where we judge
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
                to be an element of
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a:A
            %li
              %p
                Context judgments, where we judge
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
                to be a context,
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Γ
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} ctx
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \; \mathrm{ctx}
          %p
            In addition, we have a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+set"} countably
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinite+set"} infinite
            number of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inference+rules"} inference rules
            for the countably infinite number of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Russell+universes"} Russell universes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Type
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Type
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Type
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Type}_0, \mathrm{Type}_1, \mathrm{Type}_2, \ldots
            in the theory, here represented by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metavariable"} metavariable
            for conciseness:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} ctx
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %msub
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Type
                        %mi i
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Type
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma \; \mathrm{ctx}}{\Gamma \vdash \mathrm{Type}_i:\mathrm{Type}_{i + 1}}
          %p
            as well as inference rules for either
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cumulativity"} cumulativity
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lifting"} lifting
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Type
                        %mi i
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Type
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} cumul
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Type
                        %mi i
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Lift
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Type
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
